          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            %em String diagrams
            constitute a graphical calculus for expressing operations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HotzFigure2.jpg", :width => "440px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#Hotz65"} Hotz 65
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PenroseDepictAContraction.jpg", :width => "440px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#Penrose71a"} Penrose 71a
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PenroseRindlerFigureA1.jpg", :width => "440px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#PenroseRindler84"} Penrose-Rindler 84, Append.
          %p
            In the archetypical case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+monoidal+category"} Cartesian monoidal category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
            this is Hotz’s notation (
            %a{:href => "#Hotz65"}> Hotz 65
            ) for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automata"} automata
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
            with their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} usual tensor product
            this is Penrose’s notation (
            %a{:href => "#Penrose71a"}> Penrose 71a
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#PenroseRindler84"} Penrose-Rindler 84
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+networks"} tensor networks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            and yet more generally to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
          %p
            The idea is roughly to think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in a monoidal category as “strings” and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            from one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            to another as a node which the source strings enter and the target strings exit. Further structure on the monoidal category is encoded in geometrical properties on these strings.
          %p For instance:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                putting strings next to each other denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "monoidal+category#eq:TensorProductFunctor"} monoidal product
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            %li
              %p
                braiding strings over each other corresponds to – yes, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} monoidal braiding
                (if any);
            %li
              %p
                bending strings around corresponds to dualities on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+objects"} dualizable objects
                (if any).
          %p
            Many operations in monoidal categories that look unenlightening in symbols become obvious in string diagram calculus, such as the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
          %p
            String diagrams may be seen as dual (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+diagrams"} commutative diagrams
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            (shown on the left) is shown on the right here:
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %svg{:height => "120.449", :viewbox => "0 0 281.219 120.449", :width => "281.219", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
