          %p String diagrams for monoidal categories can be obtained in the same way, by considering a monoidal category as a 2-category with a single object.
          %h2#variants Variants
          %p
            There are many additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structures"} extra structures
            on monoidal categories, or similar structures, which can usually be represented by encoding further geometric properties of their string diagram calculus For instance:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in monoidal categories which are
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon categories
                the strings from above behave as if they have a small transversal extension which makes them behave as ribbons. Accordingly, there is a
                %em twist
                operation in the axioms of a ribbon category and graphically it corresponds to twisting the ribbons by 360 degrees.
            %li
              %p
                in a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+category"} traced monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                in monoidal categories which are
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+category"} spherical
                all strings behave as if drawn on a sphere.
            %li
              %p
                in a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypergraph+category"} hypergraph category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypergraphs"} hypergraphs
            %li
              %p
                string diagrams can be extended to represent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functors"} monoidal functors
                in several ways. One nice way is described in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://web.science.mq.edu.au/~mmccurdy/cms2010talk.pdf"} these slides
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/08/the_geometry_of_monoidal_fibra.html#c034428"} here
            %li
              %p
                there is also a string diagram calculus for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
                %em Poincaré dual
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe"} globular
                notation: where one uses
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2-d)
            %li
              %p
                string diagrams for bicategories can be generalized to string diagrams for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipments"} proarrow equipments
                by distinguishing between “vertical” and “horizontal” strings.
            %li
              %p
                One can also
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorify
                string diagrams to “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface+diagram"}> surface diagrams
                ”, which are dual to commutative diagrams in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-categories
                (including
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal categories
            %li
              %p
                Yet more generally, one can
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorify
                string diagrams to diagrams that are dual to commutative diagrams in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                    \-categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+diagram"}
                    manifold
                    = succeed "-diagrams" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                As explained
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://sbseminar.wordpress.com/2007/07/12/the-operadic-periodic-table/"} here
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+algebras"} planar algebras
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    canopolises
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/canopolis"} ?
            %li
              %p
                A string diagram calculus for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+fibrations"} monoidal fibrations
                can be obtained as a generalization of C.S. Peirce’s “existential graphs.” The ideas are essentially contained in (
                %a{:href => "#BradyTrimble98"}> Brady-Trimble 98
                ) and developed in (
                %a{:href => "#PontoShulman12"}> Ponto-Shulman 12
                ), and was discussed
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/08/the_geometry_of_monoidal_fibra.html"} here
            %li
              %p
                String diagrams for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
                (see also at
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelly-Mac+Lane+graph"} Kelly-Mac Lane graph
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/autonomous+categories"} autonomous categories
                %a{:href => "#BaezQG06"} here
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Rosetta"} here
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+nets"} proof nets
                used in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Lamarche08"} Lamarche
            %li
              %p
                Proof nets for multiplicative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
                with negation similarly give string diagrams for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%2A-autonomous+categories"} *-autonomous categories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+categories"} linearly distributive categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#BCST"} Blute-Cockett-Seely-Trimble
            %li
              %p
                Proof surfaces for noncommutative multiplicative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
                with negation, see
                %a{:href => "#DunnVicary17"} Dunn-Vicary
            %li
              %p
                %em> Sheet diagrams
                , string diagrams drawn on a branching surface, may be used for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+categories"} rig categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#CDH"} Comfort-Delpeuch-Hedges
          %p
            See also
            %a{:href => "#Selinger09"} Selinger 09
            for a review of different string diagram formalisms.
